1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
Chapter 7, Lecture.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Alright, let's get going.

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:11,000
I've got a lot of material to get through today.

4
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:15,000
So, let's go back to our discussion of the news media

5
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:17,000
and how people get their news.

6
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:21,000
And yeah, this turns out to be a very interesting topic

7
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:23,000
because we are seeing a significant change

8
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:26,000
in the way people get their news these days.

9
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:29,000
And it's important to note that this change,

10
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:32,000
like so many other trends we see today,

11
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:36,000
has a lot to do with the Internet.

12
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:39,000
So what I want to do today is I want to cover

13
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:41,000
several aspects of this trend

14
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:46,000
and talk about what this means for news.

15
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:49,000
Now we see the number of people actually getting the news

16
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:51,000
from print media is declining.

17
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:54,000
It's dropping.

18
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:58,000
And the Internet is quickly becoming an important source

19
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:00,000
for more and more people.

20
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,000
Okay? Great.

21
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:08,000
Now, let me move on to give you some statistics on this trend.

22
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:11,000
I want you to pay attention to this.

23
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:14,000
Researchers noticed that in 2000,

24
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:17,000
about 70% of the people they asked

25
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:19,000
said that newspapers were an important source

26
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:21,000
of information for them.

27
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:25,000
So 70% said reading the paper was important.

28
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:31,000
In 2003, that number dropped to 51%.

29
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:34,000
51%.

30
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:37,000
That's a pretty significant decline.

31
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:41,000
And I'm willing to bet that that number is even lower now.

32
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:46,000
So now I'm looking at this room full of college students.

33
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:49,000
Most of you are between 18 and 24.

34
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:52,000
And my guess is that most or maybe all of you

35
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:54,000
use the Internet.

36
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:58,000
The reason I'm talking about your age group

37
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:03,000
is that you represent the new trend away from newspapers

38
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:05,000
and toward the Internet.

39
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:07,000
You are the trend.

40
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:10,000
And experts predict that this trend away,

41
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:13,000
this trend away from newspapers,

42
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:16,000
will continue as more and more young people grow up

43
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:20,000
using the Internet to do so many things.

44
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:23,000
Let me spell this out a little more for you.

45
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:28,000
There was a key study of young people done in 2004.

46
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:33,000
And for this study, they interviewed 35 to 54-year-olds

47
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:35,000
who read the newspaper.

48
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:37,000
And they asked them if the newspaper

49
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:40,000
was an important part of their day.

50
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:44,000
Well, 38% of them said yes.

51
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:51,000
But, but, when they asked 18 to 24-year-old newspaper readers

52
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:55,000
the same question, the same question,

53
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:59,000
only 17% of them said yes.

54
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:03,000
Only 17%.

55
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:06,000
This is big news.

56
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:08,000
All right.

57
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:11,000
So to be sure, there is a move away from the traditional way

58
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:14,000
that people have gotten in use.

59
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:15,000
Okay.

60
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:19,000
Let's move on to why this trend is happening.

61
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:22,000
So why?

62
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:26,000
Now, many young people say that they prefer Internet news

63
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:29,000
because it's a fast way to keep up with events.

64
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:31,000
Now, that's probably true.

65
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:34,000
But there are a couple of other significant reasons

66
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:38,000
why news on the Internet is gaining popularity.

67
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:44,000
One is simply that news on the Internet is more up-to-date.

68
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:47,000
It's the most current information.

69
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:52,000
That's a significant difference from the news you read

70
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:55,000
in the newspaper that comes out once a day

71
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:58,000
and stays the same for 24 hours.

72
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:01,000
Now, a second feature of Internet news is

73
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:04,000
it gives people the ability to get informed

74
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:06,000
about any kind of news--

75
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:09,000
weather, road events, traffic--

76
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:13,000
news that is more in-depth than in a newspaper.

77
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:23,000
So the bottom line here is Internet news is more complete.

78
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:26,000
With an online newspaper, for example,

79
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:29,000
you can begin to read a news story,

80
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:33,000
go to a related audio or video link, and so on.

81
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:36,000
You can keep going and get more information.

82
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:40,000
Now, let's move on to another attraction of the Internet.

83
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:44,000
I want you to notice I keep talking about links.

84
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:50,000
That's because on the Internet, those links are there,

85
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:54,000
and you decide how much you want to learn,

86
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:58,000
and you decide what kind of news you want to get.

87
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:03,000
So the Internet is interactive.

88
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:10,000
In other words, there is communication

89
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:14,000
between the person looking for news and the website.

90
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:21,000
Again, you, the Internet user, control the news you get.

91
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:25,000
Now, I want to point out that this movement to Internet news

92
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:28,000
has obviously made a lot of people excited,

93
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:33,000
but it's also made some experts who study the media--

94
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:37,000
well, it's made them worried.

95
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:40,000
Some of their concerns are the flip side,

96
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:45,000
meaning the opposite of the attractions we talked about earlier.

97
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:49,000
For example, I mentioned that Internet news is fresh.

98
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:51,000
It's up to date.

99
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:58,000
Well, the downside, that is, the negative side of this,

100
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:01,000
is that reporters writing the news for the Internet

101
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:05,000
must work extremely quickly to research, write,

102
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:09,000
and get the news on the Internet as soon as possible.

103
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:15,000
So in this rush, rush, rush, to get the news onto the Internet,

104
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:18,000
it's easy for the reporters to make mistakes

105
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:23,000
or maybe leave out some important information.

106
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:27,000
So media experts are concerned that Internet news

107
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:36,000
might sometimes be less accurate and less reliable than newspaper news.

108
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:40,000
So that's one possible downside of Internet news.

109
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:46,000
Okay, now, here's another possible problem.

110
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:48,000
We said before that many Internet users

111
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:52,000
really enjoy controlling their news.

112
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:55,000
Maybe that sounded good, right?

113
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:58,000
But the worry for media experts is the possibility

114
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:02,000
that Internet users will limit their news too much.

115
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:05,000
They won't know everything they should.

116
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:09,000
Now, this is a crucial idea.

117
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:13,000
And the idea is that people who get their news on the Internet

118
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:16,000
may only learn what they want to learn

119
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:21,000
and miss other important information that they should know.

120
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:26,000
All right, now, I want to bring up another concern,

121
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:29,000
and it's related to the previous one.

122
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:34,000
It has to do with this idea of limiting what news you get on the Internet.

123
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:41,000
So, now, right now, let's take a look at this.

124
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:44,000
It's the front page of a newspaper.

125
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:47,000
It's a typical front page of a newspaper.

126
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:51,000
Here you see all kinds of news just on the front page,

127
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:55,000
international, national, local news.

128
00:07:55,000 --> 00:08:00,000
And you can also find out right away what news is most important.

129
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:03,000
The important news has a bigger headline.

130
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:06,000
Here it is, at or near the top of the page.

131
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:09,000
And maybe it's longer.

132
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:12,000
Look at this headline.

133
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:16,000
You know by where it's at that it's the most important news.

134
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:22,000
It's more important than the other news.

135
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:25,000
But when you get the news online,

136
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:29,000
you tend to see something like a list, a list, you know,

137
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:33,000
line, line, line of headlines to choose.

138
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:36,000
You don't always know what's important.

139
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:41,000
So the worry is that Internet news readers will have much less information

140
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:46,000
about the important things they need to find out in today's news.

141
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:51,000
So what I'm saying is some experts fear that online news readers

142
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:55,000
will be less informed citizens who maybe, well,

143
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:59,000
who might not participate as much in their communities.

144
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:02,000
In other words, if people know less about the world around them,

145
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:09,000
then they might not vote, say, or help make other decisions in their communities.

146
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:14,000
And they just won't be as interested in the events going on around them.

147
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:16,000
That's the fear.

148
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:20,000
All right, I see our time is almost up.

149
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:24,000
Well, I want to stress that the trend away from newspapers to the Internet

150
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:27,000
is happening right in front of us.

151
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:30,000
And as students of the media,

152
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:34,000
you may or may not agree with some of the concerns of the researchers.

153
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:38,000
But it certainly offers us a lot to think about.

154
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:41,000
Okay, I'll see you guys next time.

155
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:45,000
[music]

156
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:47,000
[music]

